% composition-series: Ontario Listening Bridges — set 1
% composition-grade: 3
% composition-elements: 3/4 metre; dotted half notes at every phrase end; mf, p and f signs; ABA form; a partner song in which group 2 sings the B line against the returning A (bars 9-12); low so and low la (C4, D4) in an F pentatonic melody
% composition-listen: Mercedes Sosa — "Gracias a la vida" (words and music by Violeta Parra, 1966), from Sosa's album "Homenaje a Violeta Parra" (Philips Argentina, 1971)
% composition-listen-url: https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Mercedes+Sosa+Gracias+a+la+vida+Homenaje+a+Violeta+Parra+1971
% composition-bridge: Notice how vocal timbre, breath and phrase shape make a repeated metre carry a public message.
% composition-context: Violeta Parra wrote "Gracias a la vida" in 1966, near the end of her life; Mercedes Sosa's 1971 recording carried it across the Spanish-speaking world and it became a defining song of nueva canción, the movement that joined folk forms to social testimony. This original partner song does not use its words, melody or harmony.
% composition-distance: No lyric, melody, rhythm or harmony from the copyrighted song is used; the classroom piece is a pentatonic partner song with new English words, and the reference is for listening and historical inquiry.
% improv: {"id":"A","bars":"3-4","role":"whole class","prompt":"Shape the long last note of the A line","choices":["hold it full","let it fade","stop on beat 3 and breathe"],"replyBars":1}
% improv: {"id":"B","bars":"9-12","role":"two groups","prompt":"Balance the partner lines","choices":["melody f, partner p","both mf","partner f, melody p"],"replyBars":4}
% dossier-function: original Grade 3 partner song and listening bridge; the class sings A, B and A in unison, then group 2 sings the B line against group 1's returning A, before comparing how Mercedes Sosa uses breath, timbre and phrase shape
% dossier-elements: twelve bars in 3/4 in ABA form. A (bars 1-4): C5-A4, D5 dotted half, C5-G4, F4 dotted half. B (bars 5-8): F4-A4-C5, F4-D4-F4, G4 half and C4, F4 dotted half. A (bars 9-12) returns with group 2 singing B underneath. F-major pentatonic pitch set F-G-A-C-D with low so (C4) and low la (D4); written range C4-D5; quarters, halves and dotted halves only; mf, p, f
% dossier-practice: Quarter note = 88 is the target; begin at 72 and breathe in the last beat before each new line. F major is the rehearsal key and the written range C4-D5 matches the young-unison C4-D5 range. Teach in steps: A by echo, B by echo, ABA in unison with the written dynamics, then half the class sings B while the other half sings the returning A; swap groups
% dossier-accompaniment: The page's ballad style (soft piano and bass) is a neutral school accompaniment chosen so the voices lead; it does not imitate Sosa's guitar and bombo arrangements or any Andean or Argentine style. A teacher may play F-Dm-C-F, one chord a bar, which fits A and B together
% dossier-lyrics: Original English words written for this record: "Sing it slow, sing it clear, / open the window, open it wide, / let it out, let it fly." Group 2 sings the B words again in bars 9-12. One syllable per note, each strong syllable on beat 1
% dossier-honesty: AI-assisted original course composition made for Grand Central Stationery on 11 November 2026 under human-directed range, grade and form constraints. It is not traditional, not collected from a community and not modelled on the melody of "Gracias a la vida"; the recording is a listening bridge only
% dossier-licence: CC0 1.0. Grand Central Stationery dedicates the melody, words, implied chords and this ABC encoding to the public domain to the extent legally possible. "Gracias a la vida" and Mercedes Sosa's recording remain the copyright of their owners and are not included
% dossier-respect: Credit Violeta Parra (Chile, 1917-1967) as the songwriter and Mercedes Sosa (Argentina, 1935-2009) as the performer, and connect the song to its Spanish words and to nueva canción's history of testimony, censorship and exile rather than treating it as background folk colour. Never present this classroom piece as nueva canción or as a Latin American song
% dossier-review: Grade 3 choir teacher, second score reader and Latin American music educator; all reviews still required
