N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition; this is the compositional audit. Every bar totals three quarters with no pickup, in both voices. Group 1: bars 1-4 C5 half, A4 | D5 dotted half | C5 half, G4 | F4 dotted half; bars 5-8 F4-A4-C5 | F4-D4-F4 | G4 half, C4 | F4 dotted half; bars 9-12 repeat bars 1-4. Group 2 rests in bars 1-8 and sings bars 5-8 in bars 9-12. Pitch set F-G-A-C-D, range C4-D5, largest leap a fifth (C5 to F4 in bars 5-6, G4 to C4 in bar 7, F4 to C5 into bar 9). Against each other the two lines sound thirds, fifths and octaves on most beats, fourths on beats 1-2 of bar 11 and a unison F to end. Form ABA, with the partner texture in the last A. Originality check: compared with "Gracias a la vida" and with common partner songs and 3/4 school songs (Lavender's Blue, Oh My Darling Clementine, Down in the Valley, Amazing Grace); the C-A-D-C-G-F arch is a common pentatonic shape and matches none of them.
N:UNCERTAIN: The analyzer reads Grade 4 only because of the one-flat key signature (every sung note is F-pentatonic, and the course's grading leaves the key out since the page transposes); a teacher should confirm the Grade 3 placement. Whether Grade 3 groups can hold B against A at 88 and whether the bar-11 fourths sound open or empty can only be settled by a classroom trial. The album details (track order and release month) and the history framing need a second reader and a Latin American music educator.
